// The 35 mm film canister of the loading screen (see pages/index.astro, styles/intro.css, game/intro.ts), as one vector
// drawing, so that its ellipses, curves, type and shading are exact.
//
// It is drawn in units, `W` across the barrel (so the radius `R` is `W / 2`). The proportions are those of a real
// cartridge (compare a photograph of one): the barrel is about 0.56 as wide as it is tall (from the middle of its top
// face to the bottom of its foot), the stub on top is a little under half the barrel's width, and the film's own slot is
// on the back of the barrel, so the film comes from behind it.
//
// It is seen from slightly above: a circle on it is an ellipse `K` times as tall as it is wide (K is the sine of the
// angle, 0.3 is about 17 degrees; 0 would be straight on). That is the top face and the knob, and the curve of the bottom
// edge and of the rings. Both ends are the same: a black ring the width of the barrel, with the flat end above the top one.
//
// The label is one flat artwork, drawn in *arc length* (how far round the can, 0 at the front), and the can's surface is
// made of thin slices of it. Slice `i` covers the angles `a0..a1`: its piece of the artwork is moved to
// `R + R sin(a0)`, stretched by the ratio of the projected distance to the arc length (about `cos(a)`), and dropped by
// `R K cos(a)`. So everything on the label, the DX checkerboard included, narrows continuously toward the sides of the
// can, its rows curve down at the front as lines round a barrel do seen from above, and the type keeps its proportions
// in the middle. Nothing is placed as a rigid block at one angle.
//
// The light: one light, which the whole page agrees on. The backdrop is brightest up-left of the loader, and the shadows
// (the film's, and this canister's own on what is behind it) fall down and to the right, so it comes from the upper left
// and from in front, as `LIGHT` below (not strong: the page is a dim brown). Everything here is lit from that one
// vector, rather than by gradients set by eye:
//   - a vertical surface (the body, the stub, the rings) is lit by `LIGHT . normal`, which for a cylinder is a function of
//     the angle round it: brightest left of the middle, and in shade over its right-hand edge. The left edge is still lit
//     (a left light reaches it at a grazing angle); nothing is dark that the light reaches.
//   - the top face is flat, and faces up, so it is lit evenly: no gradient across it.
//   - a thing that stands out casts a shadow along the light, away from it, as long as it is tall: the stub's across the top
//     face (its height over the tangent of the light's elevation, foreshortened by `K`). There is no fall-off toward the foot.
//
// What is on it is what a real canister carries: the DX code, a big black-and-white checkerboard that takes up a good
// share of the label; a barcode beside it; the speed; and a line of type. Generic on purpose: no film brand, only a small
// mark. The film in the page sits behind this drawing and comes out from behind the barrel's right edge, which is whole.
//
// The knob is the part that turns, so it is not in this drawing: it is its own element over the top, and it is a
// flip-book. Rotating a bitmap of a circle and then squashing it to the tilt's ellipse, sixty times a second, resamples
// a thin slot through a 3-to-1 minification at a different angle every frame, which shimmers. So nothing is rotated or
// squashed at run time: the knob is drawn as `KNOB_FRAMES` frames of its face, each already an ellipse with the slot at
// that frame's angle (a slot is the same every 180 degrees, so the frames cover half a turn in equal steps), in one
// strip, rastered once at the size they are shown. A window shows one frame, and the page moves the strip behind it in
// steps (`data-intro-disc`, `data-frames`), on the compositor. The turn it shows is a smooth function of time (the
// film's plan: position, speed and acceleration continuous) read to the nearest frame, so there have to be enough of
// them that the nearest is never visibly off: at one degree a frame it is within half a degree (a fifth of a pixel at
// the rim) and the turn from one animation frame to the next is as even as the plan's, to a quarter of a pixel. (At
// 24 frames it was not: 7 degrees, almost 2 pixels, a visible jitter.) The strip is the background of a plain HTML
// element, so that it is certain to be a layer of its own that the compositor moves.
const K = 0.3;
const W = 82; // the barrel's width (units)
const R = W / 2;
const RY = R * K; // the half-height of a circle of the can's radius
const TOP = 36; // y of the middle of the top face
const BODY_H = 145.2; // from there to the lowest point of the bottom edge
const SIDE_BOTTOM = TOP + BODY_H - RY; // where the straight sides end
const FOOT = 12; // the foot ring's height
const TOP_RING = 7.5; // the black ring at the top of the barrel
const RING_TOP = SIDE_BOTTOM - FOOT; // the foot ring's top edge at the sides (it curves down by RY at the front)
const SPOOL = 15; // the stub standing on the top face
const KNOB_R = 0.457 * R; // the stub's radius: 0.457 of the barrel's width across, as on the real thing
const LOWEST = TOP + BODY_H;
const MARGIN = 4; // room each side of the barrel in the drawing, for the shadow's start and the antialiasing
const MIN_Y = TOP - SPOOL - KNOB_R * K - 1; // the drawing's top edge (room for the knob's ellipse above the stub)
const KNOB_FRAMES = 180; // the knob's face, in 1 degree steps (a half turn: its slot repeats every 180)
const KNOB_W = KNOB_R * 2; // one frame's width, and (times K) its height
const VIEW_W = W + 2 * MARGIN;
const VIEW_H = LOWEST - MIN_Y;

const rad = (deg: number) => (deg * Math.PI) / 180;

// ---- the light: where it comes from, as the direction toward it (x right, y up, z toward the viewer), unit length
const LIGHT = (() => {
  const v = { x: -0.5, y: 0.6, z: 0.62 };
  const n = Math.hypot(v.x, v.y, v.z);
  return { x: v.x / n, y: v.y / n, z: v.z / n };
})();
const AMBIENT = 0.32; // what the shade still gets (light off the backdrop and the rest of the room)
const SIDE_PEAK = Math.hypot(LIGHT.x, LIGHT.z); // the brightest a vertical surface gets: where its normal faces the light
/** 0..1: how lit the side of a vertical cylinder is at the angle `deg` round it (0 facing the viewer, positive to the right). */
const lit = (deg: number) => {
  const d = LIGHT.x * Math.sin(rad(deg)) + LIGHT.z * Math.cos(rad(deg)); // light . normal (the up part does not matter)
  return Math.min(1, AMBIENT + ((1 - AMBIENT) * Math.max(0, d)) / SIDE_PEAK);
};
/** The side's gradient across its width (offsets are where `R + R sin(angle)` falls), as stops: `colour` at full light. */
const sideStops = (colour: [number, number, number]) =>
  Array.from({ length: 19 }, (_, i) => {
    const deg = -90 + (180 * i) / 18;
    const k = lit(deg);
    const rgb = colour.map((c) => Math.round(c * k));
    return { offset: ((1 + Math.sin(rad(deg))) / 2).toFixed(4), color: `rgb(${rgb[0]} ${rgb[1]} ${rgb[2]})` };
  });
/** The same, for the label, as black over it: how much of its colour the shade takes away. */
const labelShade = Array.from({ length: 19 }, (_, i) => {
  const deg = -90 + (180 * i) / 18;
  return { offset: ((1 + Math.sin(rad(deg))) / 2).toFixed(4), opacity: ((1 - lit(deg)) * 0.85).toFixed(3) };
});
const ringStops = sideStops([34, 31, 28]); // black metal, as lit: nearly the film's own black, a little light on it
const stubStops = sideStops([40, 37, 33]);
// the stub's shadow on the top face: along the light, away from it, its height over the tangent of the light's elevation
const ELEVATION_TAN = LIGHT.y / SIDE_PEAK;
const SHADOW_LEN = SPOOL / ELEVATION_TAN; // (in the plane of the face)
const SHADOW_DX = (-LIGHT.x / SIDE_PEAK) * SHADOW_LEN; // away from the light: to the right...
const SHADOW_DY = (LIGHT.z / SIDE_PEAK) * SHADOW_LEN * K; // ...and away from the viewer: up the drawing (foreshortened by K)
const shadowSteps = Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, i) => i / 5);

// the label's slices: the can's visible half, -90 to +90 degrees, in `SLICES` pieces
const SLICES = 56;
const slices = Array.from({ length: SLICES }, (_, i) => {
  const a0 = -90 + (180 * i) / SLICES;
  const a1 = -90 + (180 * (i + 1)) / SLICES;
  const u0 = R * rad(a0); // arc length at each side of it
  const u1 = R * rad(a1);
  const x0 = R + R * Math.sin(rad(a0)); // where they are seen
  const x1 = R + R * Math.sin(rad(a1));
  const mid = rad((a0 + a1) / 2);
  return { id: `fc-s${i}`, u0, u1, x0, sx: (x1 - x0) / (u1 - u0), dy: RY * Math.cos(mid) };
});

// the DX code: 2 cells wide and 6 high, which of them are dark (an irregular pattern, as the real one is)
const DX_ROWS = [
  [1, 0],
  [0, 1],
  [1, 1],
  [0, 0],
  [1, 0],
  [0, 1],
];
// It runs the whole height of the label, from the top ring to the foot ring (both edges of the label are curves that
// drop by the same amount as the artwork does at each angle, so in the flat artwork they are straight lines): its border
// goes in under the rings, and its rows share what is between them (so the cells are taller than they are wide, as a
// real code's are: a tall column of them).
const LABEL_TOP = TOP + TOP_RING; // where the label starts, under the top ring (flat artwork)
const LABEL_BOTTOM = RING_TOP; // and ends, over the foot ring
const DX_BORDER = 2; // the black border round the cells
const CELL_W = 14; // (arc length)
const CELL_H = (LABEL_BOTTOM - LABEL_TOP - 2 * DX_BORDER) / DX_ROWS.length;

// The label's lettering, as outlines of Figtree ExtraBold (SIL OFL) rather than <text>: text takes whatever font has loaded when the
// loading screen first paints (system-ui, then Figtree), so it used to change as the page loaded. Each path is centred on
// its own origin: "200" and the subtitle on the middle of their line (as text-anchor=middle, dominant-baseline=central did), the
// mark on its baseline.
